WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:03.450
- [Instructor] So I am here at desmos.com,

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.630
which is an online graphing calculator,

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.270
and the goal of this video is

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:10.430
to explore how shifts in functions happen.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.620
How do things shift to the right or left

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.120
or how do they shift up and down?

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.920
And what we're going to start off doing

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.140
is just graph a plain vanilla function,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.280
f of x is equal to x squared.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.930
That looks as we would expect it to look,

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:30.050
but now let's think about how
we can shift it up or down.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.050
Well one thought is, well, to shift it up,

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.690
we just have to make the
value of f of x higher

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.510
so we can add a value,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.360
and that does look like
it shifted it up by one.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.970
Whatever f of x was before,

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:42.400
we're now adding one to it

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.070
so it shifts the graph up by
one, that's pretty intuitive.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.190
If we subtract one,

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.790
or actually, let's subtract three.

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.190
Notice, it shifted it down.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.710
The vertex was right
over here at zero, zero.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.530
Now it is at zero, negative three,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.920
so it shifted it down.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.440
And we can set up a slider here

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.700
to make that a little bit clearer,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.150
so if I just replace this with,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.460
if I just replace this
with the variable k,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.010
then let me delete this little thing here,

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.110
that little subscript thing that happened.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.600
Then we can add a slider k here,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.320
and this is just allowing us
to set what k is equal to,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.550
so here, k is equal to one,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.430
so this is x squared plus one,

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.740
and notice, we have shifted up,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.250
and if we increase the value of k,

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.490
notice how it shifts the graph up,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.620
and as we decrease the value of k,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.240
if k is zero, we're back where our vertex

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.850
is right at the origin,

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.770
and as we decrease the value of k,

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.690
it shifts our graph down.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.120
And that's pretty intuitive,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.210
'cause we're adding or subtracting
that amount to x squared

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.730
so it changes, we could say the y value,

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.360
it shifts it up or down.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.310
But how do we shift to
the left or to the right?

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.020
So what's interesting here
is to shift to the left

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.050
or the right, we can replace our x

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.650
with an x minus something,

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.410
so let's see how that might work.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.680
So I'm gonna replace our x

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.610
with an x minus, let's replace
it with an x minus one.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:12.540
What do you think is going to happen?

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.810
Do you think that's going
to shift it one to the right

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.120
or one to the left?

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.460
So let's just put the one in.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:20.330
Well, that's interesting.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.800
Before, our vertex was at zero, zero.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.320
Now our vertex is at one, zero.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.230
So by replacing our x with an x minus one,

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.910
we actually shifted one to the right.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.440
Now why does that make sense?

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.610
Well, one way to think about it,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.160
before we put this x,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.180
before we replaced our
x with an x minus one,

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.730
the vertex was when we were squaring zero.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.740
Now, in order to square zero,

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.810
squaring zero happens
when x is equal to one.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.880
When x is equal to one,
you do one minus one,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.570
you get zero, and then that's
when you are squaring zero.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.840
So it makes sense that you
have a similar behavior

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.750
of the graph at the vertex
now when x equals one

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:05.190
as before you had when x equals zero.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.280
And to see how this can be generalized,

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.300
let's put another variable here

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.560
and let's add a slider for h.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.320
And then we can see that
when h is zero and k is zero,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.880
our function is really
then just x squared,

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.540
and then if h increases,

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:25.860
we are replacing our x with
x minus a larger value.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.090
That's shifting to the right

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.130
and that is, as h decreases,

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.460
as it becomes negative,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.930
that shifts to the left.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.450
Now right here, h is
equal to negative five.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.260
You typically won't see
x minus negative five.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.530
You would see that written as x plus five,

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:45.540
so if you replace your
x's with an x plus five,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.450
that actually shifts everything
five units to the left.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.040
And of course, we can shift both

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.820
of them together, like this.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.890
So here, we're shifting it up,

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:57.410
and then we are,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.330
we could get back to our
neutral horizontal shift

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.640
and then we can shift it
to the right like that.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.330
And everything we did just now

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:09.800
is with the x squared
function as our core function,

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.670
but you could do it with
all sorts of functions.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.970
You could do it with an
absolute value function.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.550
Let's do absolute value,
that's always a fun one.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.960
So instead of squaring all this business,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.160
let's have an absolute value here.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.430
So I'm gonna put an absolute, whoops.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.810
Absolute value,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:28.680
and there you have it.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.130
You can start at, let me
make both of these variables

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.160
equal to zero, so that
would just be the graph of

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.140
f of x is equal to the
absolute value of x.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.280
But let's say you wanted to shift it

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.500
so that this point right over
here that's at the origin

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.670
is at the point negative
five, negative five,

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.330
which is right over there.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:51.020
So what you would do is
you would replace your x

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.890
with x plus five,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:56.730
or you would make this h variable

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.260
to negative five right
over here, 'cause notice,

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:01.690
if you replace your h
with a negative five,

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:05.090
inside the absolute value,
you would have an x plus five,

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.700
and then if you want to shift it down,

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.410
you just reduce the value of k,

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:10.090
and if you want to shift it down by five,

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.150
you reduce it by five,

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.010
and you could get something like that.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.950
So I encourage you, go to desmos.com.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.230
Try this out for yourself,

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.370
and really play around
with these functions

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.990
to give yourself an
intuition of how things

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.810
and why things shift up or down

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:25.890
when you add a constant,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.070
and why things shift to
the left or the right

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.410
when you replace your x's
with an x minus, in this case,

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.490
an x minus h,

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:37.123
but it really could be x
minus some type of a constant.

